{:edb/db-before-t 1312 :edb/db-after-t 1313 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534625 50 #inst "2026-10-08T02:47:57.029Z" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096329 1100 "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ  <       \\phi <        90^\\circ$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $\\phi?$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096329 1100 "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ < \\phi < 90^\\circ$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $\\phi$?" 13194139534625 false] [17592186096329 1145 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 2; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= 1 \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (1) \\\\\n&= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534625 false] [17592186096329 1145 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096330 1100 "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R$?" 13194139534625 false] [17592186096330 1100 "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096330 1145 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x + ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ + \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x + R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = R\\sin(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = \\sqrt{2}; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= \\sqrt{2}^{2} + \\sqrt{7}^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 2 + 7 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nR &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$." 13194139534625 false] [17592186096330 1145 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x   = R\\sin(x+\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x    = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\sqrt{2}}^{2}+{\\sqrt{7}}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2+7 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096331 1100 "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left( 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534625 true] [17592186096331 1100 "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534625 false] [17592186096331 1145 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi)\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.588rad\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= (3)^{2} + (2)^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 4 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 13 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$" 13194139534625 false] [17592186096331 1145 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x    = R\\sin(x-\\phi) ,\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi )\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.588\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096332 1100 "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left( 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right).$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096332 1100 "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$." 13194139534625 false] [17592186096332 1145 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\cos (x + ϕ) &= R(\\cos x\\cos ϕ - \\sin x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\cos x - R\\sin ϕ\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -(R \\sin \\phi)\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\sin ϕ)^{2} + (R\\cos ϕ)^{2} &= 3^{2} + (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\sin^{2} ϕ + \\cos^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 3 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 12 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$" 13194139534625 false] [17592186096332 1145 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -( R \\sin \\phi )\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299578 13194139534625 true] [17592186096527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299616 13194139534625 true] [17592186096528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299571 13194139534625 true] [17592186096528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299607 13194139534625 true] [17592186096528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299623 13194139534625 true] [17592186096529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299585 13194139534625 true] [17592186096529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299592 13194139534625 true] [17592186096529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299611 13194139534625 true] [17592186096530 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299600 13194139534625 true] [17592186096883 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534625 true] [17592186096883 1100 "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186096883 1100 "Given that the expression $4\\sin x + 3\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin (x + ϕ)$ with $R > 0$, what is the value of $R$?" 13194139534625 false] [17592186096883 1145 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186097135 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534625 true] [17592186097135 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" 13194139534625 true] [17592186097135 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$\\sin x + \\sqrt{3}\\cos x =$" 13194139534625 false] [17592186097135 1145 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\